The factorial of n, as a list of decimal digits, least significant first. Each digit is multiplied and carried, so no number is ever bigger than a digit times n.
The probe is the integer limit: 25! is far past 2^53, and this program computes it exactly, since no single integer ever gets near the limit.
---
(lam n
  (letrec mul (lam d (lam m (lam c
      (if (? d)
          (if (= c 0) ~ [(mod c 10) (mul ~ m (div c 10))])
          (let t (+ (* (head d) m) c)
            [(mod t 10) (mul (tail d) m (div t 10))])))))
    (letrec fact (lam k
        (if (= k 0)
            [1 ~]
            (mul (fact (- k 1)) k 0)))
      (fact n))))
---
0 => cons 1 nil fuel 1000000
5 => cons 0 cons 2 cons 1 nil fuel 1000000
20 => cons 0 cons 0 cons 0 cons 0 cons 4 cons 6 cons 6 cons 7 cons 1 cons 8 cons 0 cons 0 cons 2 cons 0 cons 9 cons 2 cons 3 cons 4 cons 2 nil fuel 1000000
25 => cons 0 cons 0 cons 0 cons 0 cons 0 cons 0 cons 4 cons 8 cons 9 cons 5 cons 8 cons 9 cons 0 cons 3 cons 3 cons 3 cons 4 cons 0 cons 0 cons 1 cons 2 cons 1 cons 1 cons 5 cons 5 cons 1 nil fuel 1000000
